## The decision

`kotoba-lang/pattern` (new repo, registered in west): a bounded pattern
matcher whose PROGRAM IS DATA.

  kotoba/pattern_core.kotoba   the machine. Empty requiredCapabilities.
  src/pattern/compile.cljc     regex subset -> program. Host-side, portable.

A program is `{:code [[op a b] ...] :classes [[lo hi ...] ...] :fold bool}` --
inert EDN. It can be printed into a `.kotoba` module as a literal, shipped in a
resource, or read at runtime with `document-edn-read`. The machine never
parses anything, so it cannot be made to reject its input; the compiler is the
only part that can refuse, and it runs before the guest does.

## A Pike VM, not backtracking

`(a+)+b` against a page of a's is the standard denial of service, and this
workspace matches input it did not write. The run carries a LIST of live
threads deduplicated by program counter rather than a stack of choices, so
each input position is visited once.

That is measurable rather than asserted. At `--fuel 20000`, with the dedup
removed the machine dies of `doc-vector-too-large` from EIGHT a's; with it,
twenty-four answer. The parity corpus carries those long inputs on purpose --
without them it passed either way, which is a test that was not testing.

## What was verified

  216/216 against the host's own RegExp, through the ESM amu emitted, over a
  corpus drawn from the real literals plus the boundary of each construct.

Both sides run: the oracle is JavaScript's RegExp (the engine the .cljc side
actually uses), and the port is the artifact driven through programs the
compiler produced from the same strings. Breaking the dedup turns exactly the
four long-input checks red with the trap named.

Two differences are deliberate and asserted rather than discovered later:
`.` matches a newline here, and the machine is leftmost-LONGEST while JS is
leftmost-first (`a|ab` on \"ab\" answers 2, not 1).

Refused by name rather than by silence, with the count each has in the corpus:
lookahead and lookbehind (2), back references (0), non-greedy quantifiers (3),
inline flags other than a leading (?i) (5). A pattern this cannot express must
not compile to one that means something else.

## Coverage of the thing it was built for

Compiling every unique regex literal in htmldom, cssom, browser, html, css and
kiyaku: 200 scraped, 183 admitted, 7 of the refusals not patterns at all
(multi-line scrape artifacts). So 183 of 193 real ones, and what is left names
itself in frequency order: (?s)/(?m) 5, non-greedy 3, lookahead 2.

## Three things measured along the way

  * THE DEFAULT FUEL BUDGET CANNOT RUN A MATCHER. 512 per instance -- a plain
    countdown returns at n=510 -- and fuel is charged per function entry, so
    the default does not finish a six-character search. `--fuel 5000` runs a
    40-character one. The knob is the host's, which is the point of it.

  * CLOJURESCRIPT HAS NO CHARACTER TYPE. `(vec \"ab\")` yields the STRINGS \"a\"
    and \"b\" and `(int \"a\")` is 0, so a `.cljc` compiler needs an explicit
    code-point helper. Without it every literal compiled to code point 0 and
    the programs still had the right SHAPE -- the kind of wrong a structural
    test calls correct. The parity test against a real engine is what caught
    it.

  * AN ENTRY THAT CALLS THE MACHINE IS REFUSED. A `main` that calls
    `match-end-at` makes the compiler answer `{:error :value, :code
    :kotoba/internal-error, :message \"value is not a boolean\"}` at the :value
    phase, where the compile-time evaluator runs the entry. `kotoba -M check`
    passes, the same module compiles for the JS backend, and the emitted
    machine answers correctly. Raising --fuel does not change it, and it is
    deterministic over three runs. It could not be reduced to a small
    reproducer in six attempts.

    So this repo does NOT use `kotoba -M test` -- the runner evaluates every
    `test-*` through the same evaluator -- and `main` returns a constant.
    `test/entry_probe.cljs` rebuilds the failing form FROM the current source
    and goes RED the day the compiler stops refusing it, so the workaround is
    removed rather than inherited.

    That probe first reported \"the limitation is gone\" because it read only
    stdout while the diagnostic went to both streams. A probe that lies in the
    reassuring direction is worse than no probe; it now reads both and prints
    what it saw.

## What this is not

Not captures. 102 of the 308 literals use a group and many are grouping rather
than capturing; opcode 5 (`save`) is reserved and `search` answers where, not
what.

Not a consumer cutover. No port has been rewritten to use this yet. The
cheapest first consumer is `kiyaku.account`'s postal validation -- six country
formats, all admitted by the subset -- and after that htmldom's eleven.
